### Step 7: Complete the Build Report

The report started after Mode detection, and it has the sections that the user approved. In this step, add the last sections. Do this in build-new mode and in gap-fill mode.

First, list the files that you wrote in this run.

**Step review:** do the Step Review in the preamble. Section: `## Generated artifacts`. Columns: `ID | File | Purpose | Notes`.

Use the IDs `GA-1`, `GA-2` and so on, with one row for each file. In the Notes cell, write the placeholders that the file has, or "None".

Then do the "Spec Review Loop" at the end of this skill. It adds the `## Alignment review` section. Do it before the findings, because idstack adds the sections to the report in the order that you approve them.

**Step review:** do the Step Review in the preamble. Section: `## Findings`. Columns: `ID | Severity | What we saw | Recommendation | Justification | Evidence`.

Use the IDs `cogload-1`, `multimedia-1`, `placeholder-1` and so on. The findings come from cognitive-load decisions, multimedia tradeoffs, placeholders and expertise-reversal flags that changed the build. In gap-fill mode, each finding gives the upstream flag that started the generation, for example `red-team:alignment-3`.

**Step review:** do the Step Review in the preamble. Section: `## Summary`, `## Top recommendations`, `## Limitations` and `## Next steps`. Columns: `ID | Action | Justification | Evidence`.

The Summary tells what you generated, the most important design decision and the placeholders to fill in. The Limitations tell that the adaptation changes the structure, not the voice or style, and that the cognitive-load estimates are heuristic. The Next steps tell the designer to add their voice and to run `/idstack:course-quality-review`.

In the Next steps, also name each placeholder field that you used, for example `[INSTRUCTOR NAME]` and the due dates. In the Limitations, tell that the placeholder list comes from the template, not from the text around each placeholder.

Run the finish command of the Step Review.

**Writing standard check.** In this command, replace `<path>` with the path from the "Report path:" line. Then run the command.

```bash
{{IDSTACK_RESOLVE}}
if [ ! -x "$_IDSTACK/bin/idstack-ste-check" ]; then echo "STE_CHECK_MISSING: $_IDSTACK"; elif ! command -v python3 >/dev/null 2>&1; then echo "STE_CHECK_UNAVAILABLE"; else "$_IDSTACK/bin/idstack-ste-check" "<path>"; fi
```

For the result, do the steps in "How to use the standard" in the "Writing Standard (ASD-STE100)" section of the preamble. Do the check a maximum of three times.

### Step 8: Update Manifest

Save the `course_content` section to `.idstack/project.json` via `bin/idstack-manifest-merge`. The merge tool replaces only the named section, preserves every other section verbatim, validates JSON, and atomically updates the top-level `updated` timestamp.

```bash
{{IDSTACK_RESOLVE}}
"$_IDSTACK/bin/idstack-manifest-merge" --create --section course_content --payload - <<'PAYLOAD'
{
  "report_path": "<set to $_REPORT_PATH from the bash block above — e.g. .idstack/exports/<course-slug>/course-builder.md>",
  "mode": "build-new",
  "generated_at": "ISO-8601 timestamp",
  "expertise_adaptation": "novice|intermediate|advanced|mixed",
  "syllabus": "syllabus.md",
  "modules": [
    "module-01-introduction-to-topic.md",
    "module-02-core-concepts.md"
  ],
  "assessments": [
    "assessment-01-analysis-essay.md",
    "assessment-02-project-proposal.md"
  ],
  "rubrics": [
    "rubric-01-analysis-essay.md",
    "rubric-02-project-proposal.md"
  ],
  "content_dir": ".idstack/course-content/",
  "generated_files": [
    ".idstack/course-content/syllabus.md",
    ".idstack/course-content/modules/module-01-introduction-to-topic.md"
  ],
  "build_timestamp": "ISO-8601 timestamp",
  "placeholders_used": [
    "instructor_name",
    "due_dates"
  ],
  "recommended_generation_targets": []
}
PAYLOAD
```

The payload is the **flat section contents** (no enclosing `course_content` key) — the merge tool already knows the section from `--section course_content`. Wrapping the payload would double-nest. `--create` makes a canonical manifest first if `.idstack/project.json` does not exist yet (the standalone case). It never replaces a manifest that exists.

**Mode field.** Set `mode` to the mode that "Mode detection" chose: `"gap-fill"` when `import_metadata.source` is `cartridge`, `scorm` or `canvas-api` and `course_content.modules` is non-empty, otherwise `"build-new"`. In `gap-fill` mode, populate `recommended_generation_targets[]` with the per-target outcomes (`status`: `generated|deferred|declined`); in `build-new` mode, leave the array empty.

The `placeholders_used` array lists any fields where placeholder text was used because the user chose to skip or defer those details. It is a record for the designer of what still needs to be filled in. No other skill reads this field.

Use only the approved rows of the report. `generated_files` comes from the `## Generated artifacts` rows. `placeholders_used` comes from their Notes cells. `recommended_generation_targets` comes from the `## Generation targets` rows: one item for each row, with `description` (the Target cell), `source` (the Source finding cell, for example `red-team:alignment-3` or `user-request`), `status` (`generated`, `deferred` or `declined`) and `output_path` (the generated file, only when the status is `generated`).

**Fallback (if `bin/idstack-manifest-merge` is unavailable):** Read the full manifest, modify only the `course_content` section, Write back. Preserve all other sections verbatim.

After writing the manifest, confirm:

"I generated your course content. You have three outputs:

- **Read this:** <the Report path, written as a link as the Report Links section of the preamble tells you> — the build report
  with evidence-backed design choices, cognitive-load notes, and a placeholders list.
  Click the link to open it.
- Course content: `.idstack/course-content/` (the course files — review them and add
  your voice and institutional details).
- System state: `.idstack/project.json` (the manifest — for downstream skills).

**Next steps:**
- Review and edit the generated files to add your expertise and institutional voice.
- Run `/idstack:course-quality-review` to audit the course design in the manifest against QM standards, CoI presence and constructive alignment. It does not read the files in `.idstack/course-content/`.
- Run `/idstack:course-export` to package the content as an IMS Common Cartridge or push to Canvas."

